::JOUR_U04_L02_GP_Q01::[MLA.JOUR.MED.02] Source: Headline: Advisory Campaign Encourages Students to Arrive on Time. Body: The article explains that the school is starting a two-week advisory campaign about arriving on time. The central idea is that consistent arrival helps students begin class prepared. The article supports this idea with three details: the assistant principal says students miss opening instructions when they arrive late, a teacher says the first five minutes often include warm-up practice, and a student mentor says reminders in advisory can help students plan morning routines. The article ends by explaining that advisory teachers will review arrival tips each Monday. Question 1: Which statement is the central idea? {
~A. State only the broad topic without the message.#Not correct. Choice A says 'State only the broad topic without the message.', which does not fully answer this specific journalism task. Recheck the source details, the question wording, and the mapped standard before choosing the best supported answer.
=B. State the central idea accurately and explain how relevant source details develop that main message.#Correct. Step 1: identify the media literacy skill named in the standard. Step 2: compare the option with the embedded media text and lesson model. This answer works because it explains how structure, features, evidence, or central idea supports audience understanding.
~C. Choose a true detail that does not develop the central idea.#Not correct. Choice B says 'Choose a true detail that does not develop the central idea.', which does not fully answer this specific journalism task. Recheck the source details, the question wording, and the mapped standard before choosing the best supported answer.
~D. Add unsupported evidence that is not in the media text.#Not correct. Choice C says 'Add unsupported evidence that is not in the media text.', which does not fully answer this specific journalism task. Recheck the source details, the question wording, and the mapped standard before choosing the best supported answer.
}
